7810-9e9a-7f44c9eceb52.jsonl", :turn 9, :event :turn-round, :truncated? false, :message-count 2, :harvester-version 1, :reasoning-count 2, :profile :codex-rollout, :text "Codex rollout turn 9\n\nREASONING (plaintext available in rollout)\n**Clarifying Lean validation scope and encoding plan**\n**Designing formal experiment contract and invariants**\n**Defining endpoint accessibility and verification limits**\n\n**Planning Lean verification strategy**\n**Designing formal contract and claim ledger**\n\nAGENT MESSAGES\nYes—but Lean should validate the experiment’s logical contract, not attempt to prove that the scientific hypothesis is true. That distinction matters: Lean can perfectly verify a monkey-patched formalization of the wrong question.\n\nThe current problem is that prose claims, implementation details, and empirical interpretations keep drifting apart. A formal contract would have caught several failures immediately:\n\n| Required property | What Lean could check |\n|---|---|\n| Held loci are fixed | Their inherited rules never change during evaluation |\n| Linked HGT | `field[i]` and `hold[i]` always come from the same donor |\n| Paired RNG | Both damage branches consume identical indexed random tapes |\n| Correct null model | Mutation expectation matches elitism and reproduction lifecycle |\n| Static endpoint | A supplied fully held genome satisfies the raw-performance threshold |\n| Accessible path | Every step is permitted by the actual mutation/recombination relation |\n| Functional preservation | Raw performance stays above threshold along the path |\n| Genuine assimilation | Plastic dependence falls, rather than merely its reported proxy |\n| Final genetic encoding | Disabling residual rewriting preserves final performance |\n\nI would formalize a `BaldwinWitness`, roughly:\n\n```lean\nstructure BaldwinWitness where\n  path              : List Genome\n  reachable_steps   : Pairwise Accessible path\n  performance       : ∀ g ∈ path, rawFitness g ≥ threshold\n  plasticity_falls  : plasticDependence path.getLast\n                        < plasticDependence path.head\n  fixed_endpoint    : plasticDependence path.getLast = 0\n  inherited_function :\n    rawFitness (disablePlasticity path.getLast) ≥ threshold\n```\n\nThat immediately separates four questions currently being conflated:\n\n1. Can the representation encode a fixed successful solution?\n2. Is that solution reachable using the declared genetic operators?\n3. Is there a function-preserving path toward it?\n4. Did the observed evolutionary run actually follow such a path?\n\nThe first three are design gates. The fourth is empirical.\n\nA useful Lean development would have four layers:\n\n- **Semantics:** genome, phenotype, rewrite step, hold, mask, gamma, update probability, damage intervention and random tape.\n- **Genetic operators:** mutation, selection and HGT, with linkage and lifecycle invariants.\n- **Scientific definitions:** raw function, actual plastic dependence, static endpoint, accessible path and Baldwin witness.\n- **Certificates:** externally generated candidate endpoints, paths and run traces that Lean checks against the reference semantics.\n\nFor example, the current HGT implementation would fail a theorem like:\n\n```lean\ntheorem hgt_preserves_field_hold_linkage :\n  ∀ i, child.field i = parentA.field i ∧ child.hold i = parentA.hold i\n     ∨ child.field i = parentB.field i ∧ child.hold i = parentB.hold i\n```\n\nIt transfers `field` with `mask`, not `hold`, so no such proof could be completed. Similarly, encoding elitist reproduction would have forced the correct mutation-only recurrence:\n\n\\[\nh_{t+1}=h_t+\\frac{\\mu}{2}(1-2h_t),\n\\]\n\nrather than the recurrence Claude used for mutating the entire population.\n\nThere are limits:\n\n- Lean cannot decide whether the cost represents the biologically appropriate notion of plasticity; that remains a modelling judgment.\n- It cannot ensure that the chosen definition captures “the Baldwin effect” unless we first agree on that definition.\n- Proving exhaustive endpoint existence over \\(256^{80}\\) fields is unrealistic. Lean can verify explicit endpoint and path certificates.\n- A Lean reference model does not automatically prove that the Clojure implementation conforms to it.\n\nFor the last problem, I would make the Lean model an executable oracle and require differential trace tests: given the same genome, environment and indexed random tape, Lean and Clojure must emit identical state traces and measurements. Every production run should include the model version, parameter manifest, RNG schedule and checked certificate.\n\nThe smallest valuable first milestone is not formalizing the whole CA. It is a compact “design kernel” proving:\n\n1. held rules never rewrite;\n2. HGT preserves `field`/`hold` linkage;\n3. paired branches remain tape-aligned;\n4. the null mutation recurrence matches the actual lifecycle;\n5. any reported Baldwin result contains a checked static endpoint and accessible path certificate.\n\nOnly after those theorems pass should another large selection run be authorized.\n\nThis would not eliminate scientific mistakes, but it would stop quiet semantic drift. A change such as adding `hold` would create new unproved obligations instead of silently leaving HGT, cost, and the null calculation inconsistent. That is exactly the protection the current process lacks.\n\nYes—but Lean should validate the experiment’s logical contract, not attempt to prove that the scientific hypothesis is true. That distinction matters: Lean can perfectly verify a monkey-patched formalization of the wrong question.\n\nThe current problem is that prose claims, implementation details, and empirical interpretations keep drifting apart. A formal contract would have caught several failures immediately:\n\n| Required property | What Lean could check |\n|---|---|\n| Held loci are fixed | Their inherited rules never change during evaluation |\n| Linked HGT | `field[i]` and `hold[i]` always come from the same donor |\n| Paired RNG | Both damage branches consume identical indexed random tapes |\n| Correct null model | Mutation expectation matches elitism and reproduction lifecycle |\n| Static endpoint | A supplied fully held genome satisfies the raw-performance threshold |\n| Accessible path | Every step is permitted by the actual mutation/recombination relation |\n| Functional preservation | Raw performance stays above threshold along the path |\n| Genuine assimilation | Plastic dependence falls, rather than merely its reported proxy |\n| Final genetic encoding | Disabling residual rewriting preserves final performance |\n\nI would formalize a `BaldwinWitness`, roughly:\n\n```lean\nstructure BaldwinWitness where\n  path              : List Genome\n  reachable_steps   : Pairwise Accessible path\n  performance       : ∀ g ∈ path, rawFitness g ≥ threshold\n  plasticity_falls  : plasticDependence path.getLast\n                        < plasticDependence path.head\n  fixed_endpoint    : plasticDependence path.getLast = 0\n  inherited_function :\n    rawFitness (disablePlasticity path.getLast) ≥ threshold\n```\n\nThat immediately separates four questions currently being conflated:\n\n1. Can the representation encode a fixed successful solution?\n2. Is that solution reachable using the declared genetic operators?\n3. Is there a function-preserving path toward it?\n4. Did the observed evolutionary run actually follow such a path?\n\nThe first three are design gates. The fourth is empirical.\n\nA useful Lean development would have four layers:\n\n- **Semantics:** genome, phenotype, rewrite step, hold, mask, gamma, update probability, damage intervention and random tape.\n- **Genetic operators:** mutation, selection and HGT, with linkage and lifecycle invariants.\n- **Scientific definitions:** raw function, actual plastic dependence, static endpoint, accessible path and Baldwin witness.\n- **Certificates:** externally generated candidate endpoints, paths and run traces that Lean checks against the reference semantics.\n\nFor example, the current HGT implementation would fail a theorem like:\n\n```lean\ntheorem hgt_preserves_field_hold_linkage :\n  ∀ i, child.field i = parentA.field i ∧ child.hold i = parentA.hold i\n     ∨ child.field i = parentB.field i ∧ child.hold i = parentB.hold i\n```\n\nIt transfers `field` with `mask`, not `hold`, so no such proof could be completed. Similarly, encoding elitist reproduction would have forced the correct mutation-only recurrence:\n\n\\[\nh_{t+1}=h_t+\\frac{\\mu}{2}(1-2h_t),\n\\]\n\nrather than the recurrence Claude used for mutating the entire population.\n\nThere are limits:\n\n- Lean cannot decide whether the cost represents the biologically appropriate notion of plasticity; that remains a modelling judgment.\n- It cannot ensure that the chosen definition captures “the Baldwin effect” unless we first agree on that definition.\n- Proving exhaustive endpoint existence over \\(256^{80}\\) fields is unrealistic. Lean can verify explicit endpoint and path certificates.\n- A Lean reference model does not automatically prove that the Clojure implementation conforms to it.\n\nFor the last problem, I would make the Lean model an executable oracle and require differential trace tests: given the same genome, environment and indexed random tape, Lean and Clojure must emit identical state traces and measurements. Every production run should include the model version, parameter manifest, RNG schedule and checked certificate.\n\nThe smallest valuable first milestone is not formalizing the whole CA. It is a compact “design kernel” proving:\n\n1. held rules never rewrite;\n2. HGT preserves `field`/`hold` linkage;\n3. paired branches remain tape-aligned;\n4. the null mutation recurrence matches the actual lifecycle;\n5. any reported Baldwin result contains a checked static endpoint and accessible path certificate.\n\nOnly after those theorems pass should another large selection run be authorized.\n\nThis would not eliminate scientific mistakes, but it would stop quiet semantic drift. A change such as adding `hold` would create new unproved obligations instead of silently leaving HGT, cost, and the null calculation inconsistent. That is exactly the protection the current process lacks.\n\nTOOL DIGEST\n[none]"}, :session-id "019fb3b7-23ed-7810-9e9a-7f44c9eceb52", :tags [:codex :turn-round :codex-rollout :codex-1], :at "2026-07-30T17:31:12.143Z", :type :coordination, :subject #:ref{:type :agent, :id "codex-1"}, :author "codex-1", :claim-type :step, :id "e-codexroll-019fb3b7-t009"} #:evidence{:body {:session-uuid "019fb3b7-23ed-7810-9e9a-7f44c9eceb52", :turn-id "019fb416-5cff-7c03-b5a8-73a1466fe8ce", :event-seq-range [210 310], :reasoning-encrypted-only-count 1, :tool-digest-count 32, :rollout-file "/home/joe/.codex/sessions/2026/07/30/rollout-2026-07-30T16-49-11-019fb3b7-23ed-7810-9e9a-7f44c9eceb52.jsonl", :turn 10, :event :turn-round, :truncated? false, :message-count 10, :harvester-version 1, :reasoning-count 16, :profile :codex-rollout, :text "Codex rollout turn 10\n\nREASONING (plaintext available in rollout)\n**Planning agent inspection**\n\n**Inspecting MetaCA imports in codebase**\n\n**Investigating bottom dynamics and RuleByte**\n\n**Designing generic Genome structure and rewrite rules**\n**Defining BaldwinWitness structure with genome accessibility path**\n\n**Planning genome performance modeling**\n**Formalizing expected held theorem**\n\n**Planning validated design structure**\n**Verifying Lean version and style**\n\n**Drafting Genome structure and rewriteStep definition**\n**Refining linkedHGT definition using decidable Prop chooser**\n**Adding Design structure with performance and accessibility**\n\n**Defining fixedEndpoint and geneticFunction helper**\n**Implementing mutationOnly recursion and theorems**\n\n**Designing MetaCAExperiment structure**\n\n**Validating Lean environment with lake**\n\n**Implementing polling wit