ds five audit obligations beyond the original kernel: RNG tape alignment, neutral-position fidelity, mutation reachability, non-degenerate selected axes, and treatment separation. The Clojure code appears to satisfy tape alignment and the repaired HGT linkage locally, but the run output currently cannot certify a `BaldwinWitness` because it discards genomes and ancestry. I’m checking whether separate tests or artifacts discharge the remaining obligations.\n\nThe expanded Lean specification itself checks cleanly, and the Clojure script passes static lint. The repository test suite is still running; notably, its discovered tests contain no Baldwin-selection test, so a green suite will not by itself discharge the new invariants.\n\nThe expanded Lean specification itself checks cleanly, and the Clojure script passes static lint. The repository test suite is still running; notably, its discovered tests contain no Baldwin-selection test, so a green suite will not by itself discharge the new invariants.\n\nThe focused probes passed three local mechanics: all three RNG streams consumed exactly 80 draws under opposite gate/hold settings; repaired HGT kept `field`, `mask`, and `hold` from the same donor across 100 seeded cuts; and mutation reached all declared discrete domains. A production-protocol all-held rule-90 field also scored nonzero. The remaining failures are architectural: the Clojure genome and observables do not refine the Lean types, no empirical-null or landscape gate exists, and the calibration boundary mismatch makes “successful” scientifically ambiguous.\n\nThe focused probes passed three local mechanics: all three RNG streams consumed exactly 80 draws under opposite gate/hold settings; repaired HGT kept `field`, `mask`, and `hold` from the same donor across 100 seeded cuts; and mutation reached all declared discrete domains. A production-protocol all-held rule-90 field also scored nonzero. The remaining failures are architectural: the Clojure genome and observables do not refine the Lean types, no empirical-null or landscape gate exists, and the calibration boundary mismatch makes “successful” scientifically ambiguous.\n\nA concurrent edit added an uncommitted `preflight` while I was auditing. It does not yet implement the model’s gate: it checks only I2/I3/I4/I6, and three checks are too weak—presence of HGT keys does not prove linked cuts, mutation reachability omits `field`/`mask`/`hold`, and “more than one distinct score” explicitly accepts the forbidden single-spike landscape. It also provides `--skip-preflight`, which bypasses the claimed invariant gate. I’m rechecking the latest file rather than reporting against the earlier snapshot.\n\nA concurrent edit added an uncommitted `preflight` while I was auditing. It does not yet implement the model’s gate: it checks only I2/I3/I4/I6, and three checks are too weak—presence of HGT keys does not prove linked cuts, mutation reachability omits `field`/`mask`/`hold`, and “more than one distinct score” explicitly accepts the forbidden single-spike landscape. It also provides `--skip-preflight`, which bypasses the claimed invariant gate. I’m rechecking the latest file rather than reporting against the earlier snapshot.\n\nNo—the current Clojure system should not run a new Baldwin experiment yet. Several local mechanics now match the Lean model, but the full experiment does not, and the new preflight can incorrectly certify invalid designs.\n\nI audited Clojure HEAD `d1acc34bd6bd1d9cef511db07fac9035dbf40589` plus the current uncommitted 55-line preflight addition.\n\n### What passes\n\n| Obligation | Verdict | Evidence |\n|---|---|---|\n| Held loci never rewrite | Pass | The rewrite guard returns `centre` whenever `hold[i]` is true: [baldwin_selection.clj:76](/home/joe/code/mmca-clj/scripts/baldwin_selection.clj:76) |\n| HGT linkage | Pass in implementation | `field`, `mask`, and `hold` now use the same splice: [baldwin_selection.clj:157](/home/joe/code/mmca-clj/scripts/bal